Fixes GPU hangs in Ratchet & Clank that are caused by the Nvidia driver miscompiling the fallback compute shader for GPU decompression. Compared to a working GLSL version of that fallback shader, using `VK_NV_memory_decompression` also yields better throughput (by around 10-15% on my mobile RTX 2060).

### Data layouts
Compressed data is organized into multiple tile streams (which will roughly correspond to one larger GPU resource or subresource), with the so-called control buffer storing stream metadata as follows:
```c
struct stream_t {
  uint32_t src_offset; /* relative to the start of the input buffer */
  uint32_t dst_offset; /* relative to the start of the output buffer */
};

struct control_buffer_t {
  uint32_t stream_count;
  stream_t streams[stream_count];
};
```

Each stream then starts with a metadata header of the following format:

```c
struct tile_stream_t {
  uint16_t unknown; /* always 0x01fe? */
  uint16_t tile_count;
  uint32_t frog; /* stores the uncompressed size of the last tile in bit 2 onwards */
  uint32_t last_tile_compressed_size;
  uint32_t tile_offsets[tile_count - 1]; /* relative to the end of the metadata section, for tile 1 onward */
};
```

This is in contrast to `VK_NV_memory_decompression`, which expects a flat array of the following structure *for each tile*:
```c
struct region_t {
  uint64_t src_va;
  uint64_t dst_va;
  uint64_t compressed_size;
  uint64_t decompressed_size;
  uint64_t method;
};
```

In order to resolve this mismatch, we dispatch compute shaders to parse each tile stream header and compute the appropriate address ranges. Tiles other than the last one in each stream always have a decompressed size of 64k.

The elephant in the room is that we have no way of knowing the number of tiles to decompress in advance, only the stream count and buffer addresses. In order to resolve this, we basically have two options:
1. Request a fixed-size scratch buffer large enough to hold memory region data for the maximum number of tiles in one stream (65535), and run one iteration of the decompression command per stream.
2. Request a fixed-size scratch buffer and write the entire memory region array to it, and just hope it won't overflow.

Option 1, while technically more correct, is very slow in practice and only allows for a throughput of ~1.2 GB/s in the bulk load demo on my mobile RTX 2060, compared to ~5.0 GB/s when using Option 2. Therefore, this implementation uses the latter approach, allowing for up to 65536 tiles per call, which corresponds to a maximum output data size of 4 GiB per call while the scratch buffer is roughly 2.5 MB in size.

The assumption that applications will not attempt to decode more than 4GiB of data at once seems reasonable for a number of reasons:
- All output data will be written to the same ID3D12Resource since there is no real BDA support in D3D12, and stream descriptions use offsets into that buffer.
- When streaming in textures, output data must further be copied to actual image resources, which means that a very large output buffer is essentially wasted memory.
- DirectStorage defaults to a staging buffer size of 32 MiB.

### Undocumented APIs
While D3D12 exposes a limited amount of information about meta commands, they are not publicly documented, and this implementation is mostly based on observed behaviour from invoking the command on AMD's Windows driver, as well as testing interactions with DirectStorage itself by running the [BulkLoadDemo](https://github.com/microsoft/DirectStorage/blob/main/Samples/BulkLoadDemo/README.md) on top of vkd3d-proton.

As such, the `D3D12_FEATURE_DATA_QUERY_META_COMMAND` part remains a mystery. The API consists of two opaque blobs, and I was only able to work out the meanig of some of the fields based on DirectStorage behaviour. This is a problem for the decompression test: Nvidia's Windows driver will set one of the unknown fields to 1, and attempting to run the test will cause a crash. It is not clear how applications are expected to respond to that particular bit.

I did not include a test for querying `D3D12_FEATURE_DATA_QUERY_META_COMMAND` since behaviour on Windows drivers varies greatly; there is no consistency at all except for a small set of known-good inputs. When passing invalid inputs, AMD will often return errors and Nvidia will often crash, but Nvidia also accepts some inputs that cause errors on AMD. It does not seem overly useful to test behaviour there, especially as long as we do not understand most of the data structure.

### Fallback shader
I also considered adding a custom fallback shader for systems that do not support `VK_NV_memory_decompression`, and testing showed a roughly 15% increase in throughput on the Steam Deck compared to the built-in shader from DirectStorage, while performance on my desktop 6900XT is unaffected. Unfortunately, we cannot ship this for the time being since the GDeflate reference implementation it is based on is licensed under Apache 2.0, so I removed it again.
